Working of partition-wise data storage
 - Range partitioning: each partition holds a range of salary values. p0 = salary below 30000, p1 =
   30000 to 59999, p2 = 60000 to 99999, p3 = 100000 and above.
 - When a row is inserted, MySQL applies the partition rule to the Salary value and stores the row in
   the matching partition. The user does not choose it.
 - Each partition is stored like a separate small table (its own data segment), but the whole thing is
   one logical table named Employee.
 - Partition pruning: a query such as WHERE Salary > 100000 reads only partition p3, so it is
   faster than scanning all rows.
 - Hash partitioning alternative: PARTITION BY HASH(Emp_ID) PARTITIONS 4 spreads rows
   evenly using Emp_ID mod 4.
 - Maintenance is easy: old data can be removed with ALTER TABLE Employee DROP
   PARTITION p0.
Conclusion: partitioning splits one big table into smaller physical parts by a rule on a column. The
user still writes normal queries on Employee, MySQL decides which partition stores each row, and
queries that name a partition (or filter on Salary) read only that part.


