Q. 7. Explain the Star Schema with a suitable example of a library or hospital
database. Identify the fact table and any three dimension tables.
Definition
A star schema is the simplest data warehouse design. It has one central fact table that stores
measurable values (quantity, amount) and foreign keys, surrounded by dimension tables that
describe the facts (who, what, when). Drawn out, the fact table is in the centre and the dimensions
around it, like a star.

Example: Library database
Fact table: Fact_Book_Issue. Dimension tables: Dim_Book, Dim_Member, Dim_Date.
Star schema diagram:
      [       Dim_Book      ]       [            Dim_Date      ]
               \                            /
                 +------------------+
                 | Fact_Book_Issue |
                 +------------------+
                          |
                     [    Dim_Member         ]

Fact table: Fact_Book_Issue
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Issue_ID                      INT (PK)                 Unique id of each book issue
  Book_ID                       INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Book
  Member_ID                     INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Member
  Date_ID                       INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Date
  Days_Borrowed                 INT                      Measure: days the book was kept
  Fine_Amount                   DECIMAL(6,2)             Measure: fine charged

Dimension table: Dim_Book
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Book_ID                       INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Title                         VARCHAR(50)              Book title
  Author                        VARCHAR(30)              Author
  Category                      VARCHAR(20)              Subject category

Sample rows:
  +---------+--------------------------+---------------+-------------+
  | Book_ID | Title                    | Author        | Category    |
  +---------+--------------------------+---------------+-------------+
  | 1       | Database System Concepts | Silberschatz | Database     |
  | 2       | Clean Code               | Robert Martin | Programming |
  | 3       | The Alchemist            | Paulo Coelho | Fiction      |
  +---------+--------------------------+---------------+-------------+

Dimension table: Dim_Member
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Member_ID                     INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Member_Name                   VARCHAR(30)              Member name
  Department                    VARCHAR(20)              Department
  City                          VARCHAR(20)              City

Sample rows:
  +-----------+-------------+------------+--------+
  | Member_ID | Member_Name | Department | City   |
  +-----------+-------------+------------+--------+
  | 1         | Ravi        | IT         | Pune   |
  | 2         | Priya       | CS         | Mumbai |
  | 3         | Amit        | IT         | Nashik |
  +-----------+-------------+------------+--------+

Dimension table: Dim_Date
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Date_ID                       INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Full_Date                     DATE                     Calendar date
  Month                         VARCHAR(10)              Month name
  Year                          INT                      Year

Sample rows:
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+
  | Date_ID | Full_Date | Month      | Year |
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+
  | 1       | 2026-08-05 | August    | 2026 |
  | 2       | 2026-09-10 | September | 2026 |
  | 3       | 2026-10-12 | October   | 2026 |
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+

Why star schema is useful
 - Simple design, easy for business users to understand.
 - Fast queries because only one join per dimension is needed.
 - Works well with OLAP operations (roll-up, drill-down, slice and dice).
 - Dimension tables can be reused across many fact tables.
Conclusion: in the Library star schema, Fact_Book_Issue is the fact table and Dim_Book,
Dim_Member, Dim_Date are the dimension tables joined to it by foreign keys.
Sample fact rows: (1, Book 1, Member 1, Date 1, 14 days, 0.00), (2, Book 2, Member 2, Date 2, 20
days, 30.00), (3, Book 3, Member 3, Date 3, 10 days, 0.00).


